# Instructions for AI coding agents

This is **{{productDisplayName}}**, generated from the TrustOS `{{templateId}}`
template (v{{templateVersion}}) on the TrustOS framework v{{frameworkVersion}}.

Read this file before your first change. The rules below are not style
preferences — they are the reason this application can hold other people's data.

---

## 1. Use the existing TrustOS packages

Before writing anything, check whether the capability already exists:

| Need | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Read an environment variable | `@trustos/config` — never `process.env` |
| Log anything | `@trustos/logging` — never `console.*` |
| Throw an API error | `@trustos/errors` — `ApiError.*` |
| Validate input | `@trustos/validation` — Zod + `parseOrThrow` |
| Hash a password, issue a token | `@trustos/auth` |
| Check a permission | `@trustos/rbac` |
| Scope a query to an organization | `@trustos/tenancy` |
| Record a sensitive action | `@trustos/audit` |
| Add a health check or metric | `@trustos/observability` |

## 2. Do not duplicate shared modules

No second logger, no second error shape, no second way to check a permission,
no hand-rolled validation. If a framework package is *almost* right, raise it
with the framework team — do not write a local variant. Two ways to do the same
security-relevant thing means one of them will be wrong and nobody will notice.

## 3. Preserve tenant isolation

- Every query against a tenant-owned model carries `organizationId`. Prefer
  `scopedDelegate`, which makes it structural.
- The organization comes from the **access token**, never from a request body,
  path or header.
- A lookup by primary key must pass through `assertTenantMatch`, which reports
  another tenant's row as `not_found` — never `forbidden`, which would confirm
  the id exists.
- Filtering in the frontend is never a control.

## 4. Preserve RBAC

- Every route declares `@RequirePermissions`, `@RequireRoles`,
  `@AllowAnyAuthenticated` or `@Public`. A route that declares nothing is denied.
- Never add `@Public()` to a route that handles real data.
- Never widen a role's permissions to make something work.
- Never remove a guard from `app.module.ts` or change the guard order. That
  order — authenticate, scope, authorize — is the security model.

## 4b. Preserve identity and credential handling

These are the rules that keep the identity layer honest. Each one describes a change
that compiles, passes an unmodified test suite, and quietly removes a control.

- **Never bypass identity validation.** No route reads a user id from a header, a
  query parameter or a body field. It comes from the verified actor.
- **Never decode a token without verifying its signature.** `jwt.decode` is not
  authentication. If a claim matters, the token must have been verified first.
- **Always validate the issuer and the audience.** A correctly signed token from a
  different issuer, or intended for a different audience, is somebody else's token.
- **Never trust client-supplied roles, permissions or scopes.** They are resolved on
  the server, per request, from the membership tables — never read from a claim.
- **Never trust a client-supplied organization id.** The organization comes from the
  verified actor and the server-side membership check. A header or body field naming
  an organization is an attempt, not a fact.
- **Default authorization decisions to deny.** A new policy that cannot decide returns
  "no opinion", which means deny. Never write a policy that allows on an unknown case.
- **Never store a plaintext API key or credential.** Only a prefix, a hash and
  metadata. The plaintext exists for the lifetime of one response.
- **Never log a token, a credential or a password.** Not at debug level, not
  temporarily, not "while investigating". Log the prefix or a correlation hash.
- **Preserve refresh-token reuse detection.** A used refresh token presented again
  revokes the entire family. Removing that turns a stolen refresh token into a
  permanent session.
- **Add negative tests.** A test that a valid token works proves nothing about a
  tampered one. Every new credential path needs a test that the wrong input is refused.
- **Document new permissions and scopes** in the permission catalog, with a
  description, before using them.
- **Audit every privileged operation** — credential creation, revocation, rotation,
  role changes, session revocation.

If a change requires breaking one of these, stop and ask. Do not work around it.

## 5. Add audit logging for sensitive actions

Any change to authentication state, membership, roles, permissions,
organizations, configuration, or {{#if templateEntities}}{{#each templateEntities}}`{{this}}`{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{else}}product data{{/if}}
is recorded with `AuditService.record` or `recordChange`, including `before` and
`after`. If a reviewer cannot answer "who did this and when" from the trail, the
change is not finished.

## 6. Add tests for security-sensitive changes

Anything touching authentication, authorization, tenancy, audit, error
disclosure or secret handling ships with tests in the same change. Assert the
**negative** case: the wrong caller is refused, the foreign row is invisible,
the secret is absent from the payload.

Copy the shape from the tenant-isolation spec beside the product module.

## 7. Document schema changes

A Prisma change requires a generated migration (`npm run db:migrate -- --name
what_changed`) and a note in the PR saying what changed and whether it is
backward compatible. Never hand-edit a generated migration.

Product models go in `prisma/schema/10-product.prisma`. Do **not** edit
`00-framework.prisma` — it is the framework's copy and is replaced on upgrade.

Every tenant-owned model needs `organizationId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`,
`deletedAt` and an index on `organizationId`.

## 8. Do not expose secrets

- Never log or return a password, hash, token, key or connection string.
- Never add a secret to `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` — those are compiled into the browser
  bundle.
- Never commit a `.env`. Update `.env.example` instead, with a placeholder.
- Production error responses must not carry a message, stack or class from an
  unexpected error.

## 9. Do not add unapproved infrastructure

Out of scope without explicit approval: Kafka or any message broker,
Kubernetes, microservices, Redis, GraphQL, WebSockets, a second database, an
external secrets manager, or a new cloud provider.

